PathwayData.put("data/grammar_vocabulary/units/L06U1.json",{"strand":"grammar_vocabulary","level":6,"cefr":"B1b","unit":1,"theme":"Travel and Culture","essential_question":"What can we learn from visiting other places and cultures?","lessons":[{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS1","title":"The Guide Who Spoke Arabic: Defining Relative Clauses","objectives":["Students will be able to use who, which, that, where and whose to give essential information about people, things and places.","Students will be able to choose the correct relative pronoun for people, things, places and possession.","I can say \"Our guide was a man who spoke Arabic.\"","I can say \"It's a market where you can buy spices.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام who وwhich وthat وwhere وwhose لإعطاء معلومات أساسية عن الأشخاص والأشياء والأماكن.","سيتمكن الطلاب من اختيار ضمير الوصل المناسب للأشخاص والأشياء والأماكن والملكية.","أستطيع أن أقول Our guide was a man who spoke Arabic.","أستطيع أن أقول It's a market where you can buy spices."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"destination","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌdɛstəˈneɪʃən/","definition":"the place where someone is going","arabic":"وِجهة","example":"Abha is a popular summer destination.","image_id":"GV-L06-U1-LS1-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS1-W01"},{"word":"journey","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈdʒɝni/","definition":"traveling from one place to another, often a long way","arabic":"رحلة (انتقال من مكان لآخر)","example":"The train journey from Riyadh to Dammam is comfortable.","image_id":"GV-L06-U1-LS1-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS1-W02"},{"word":"landmark","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈlændˌmɑrk/","definition":"a famous building or place that is easy to recognize","arabic":"معلم بارز","example":"The Kingdom Centre is a Riyadh landmark.","image_id":"GV-L06-U1-LS1-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS1-W03"},{"word":"souvenir","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌsuvəˈnɪr/","definition":"something you buy to remember a place","arabic":"تذكار","example":"I bought a small wooden camel as a souvenir.","image_id":"GV-L06-U1-LS1-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS1-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"A defining relative clause gives essential information about a noun – it tells us which person, thing or place we mean: Our guide was a man who spoke Arabic. Without the clause, the sentence is not clear.\nUse who for people, which for things and animals, and that for people or things (very common in speaking). Use where for places (= in/at which) and whose for possession (= his/her/their): a family whose house was next to a rainforest.\nThe relative pronoun replaces a pronoun – don't repeat it: a man who (not: he) spoke Arabic. No commas are used with defining relative clauses.\nwho → people • which → things/animals • that → people or things\nwhere → places (a market where you can…)\nwhose → possession (a family whose house…)\nDon't repeat the subject/object: (not: who he), (not: which it)","examples":["A tourist is a person who visits a place for pleasure.","The Kingdom Centre is a landmark which has a sky bridge.","This is the airport where my uncle works.","I have a friend whose father is a pilot.","The plane that leaves at nine is full.","Al-Ula is a place where you can see ancient tombs.","The souvenir which I liked most was a small lantern.","She's the travel agent who booked our tickets."],"common_errors":["✗ I met a guide which spoke Arabic. → ✓ I met a guide who spoke Arabic. (People → who (or that).)","✗ This is the hotel which we stayed. → ✓ This is the hotel where we stayed. (Place → where.)","✗ The boy who his bag is lost is crying. → ✓ The boy whose bag is lost is crying. (Possession → whose.)","✗ I bought a scarf which it was very soft. → ✓ I bought a scarf which was very soft. (Don't repeat it.)"],"arabic":"جملة الصلة المحدِّدة تعطي معلومة أساسية تحدد أي شخص أو شيء أو مكان نقصد: a man who spoke Arabic.\nwho للأشخاص، وwhich للأشياء والحيوانات، وthat للأشخاص والأشياء، وwhere للأماكن، وwhose للملكية.\nفي العربية نقول «الرجل الذي هو يتكلم»، أما في الإنجليزية فلا نكرر الضمير: a man who spoke (وليس who he spoke).\nلا نضع فواصل مع جمل الصلة المحدِّدة."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “The Guide Who Spoke Arabic: Defining Relative Clauses”. Let’s learn defining relative clauses with who, which, that, where and whose together.","on_screen":"GV-L06-U1-LS1 | The Guide Who Spoke Arabic: Defining Relative Clauses","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Listen to the start of “Omar's Travel Blog: Kuala Lumpur”: Last summer, my family traveled to Kuala Lumpur, the capital of Malaysia. It's a destination that many Saudi families love. Look at the green words.","on_screen":"Last summer, my family traveled to Kuala Lumpur, the capital of Malaysia. It's a destination that many Saudi families love.","visual":"Image GV-L06-U1-LS1-IMG01 animates gently; target words glow Leaf Green as they are spoken."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"A defining relative clause gives essential information about a noun – it tells us which person, thing or place we mean: Our guide was a man who spoke Arabic. Without the clause, the sentence is not clear. Use who for people, which for things and animals, and that for people or things (very common in speaking). Use where for places (= in/at which) and whose for possession (= his/her/their): a family whose house was next to a rainforest.","on_screen":"who → people • which → things/animals • that → people or things • where → places (a market where you can…) • whose → possession (a family whose house…)","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. A tourist is a person who visits a place for pleasure. The Kingdom Centre is a landmark which has a sky bridge. This is the airport where my uncle works.","on_screen":"A tourist is a person who visits a place for pleasure. / The Kingdom Centre is a landmark which has a sky bridge. / This is the airport where my uncle works.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “I met a guide which spoke Arabic.” That’s not right. We say: “I met a guide who spoke Arabic.” People → who (or that).","on_screen":"✗ I met a guide which spoke Arabic.   ✓ I met a guide who spoke Arabic.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:05","narration":"Quick check: \"a family whose house was next to a rainforest\" – whose house? … Think! … The family's house. Great job! Now you can say: I can say \"Our guide was a man who spoke Arabic.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can say \"Our guide was a man who spoke Arabic.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: defining relative clauses with who, which, that, where and whose.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","جملة الصلة المحدِّدة تعطي معلومة أساسية تحدد أي شخص أو شيء أو مكان نقصد: a man who spoke Arabic.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: I met a guide which spoke Arabic. — الصحيح: I met a guide who spoke Arabic.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أقول Our guide was a man who spoke Arabic."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Omar's Travel Blog: Kuala Lumpur","body":"Last summer, my family traveled to Kuala Lumpur, the capital of Malaysia. It's a destination that many Saudi families love.\nOn the first day, we visited the Petronas Twin Towers, a landmark which you can see from almost everywhere in the city. We walked across the bridge that connects the two towers – it was about 170 meters above the street!\nOur guide was a young man who spoke Arabic very well. He took us to a market where you can find spices, colorful batik cloth and souvenirs.\nOn the second day, we visited a museum which has beautiful models of famous mosques from around the world. In a park near the museum, monkeys that live in the trees tried to take my sister's snack!\nWe also tried the local cuisine. My favorite dish was nasi lemak, a meal which is made with coconut rice.\nFor two nights, we stayed with a family whose house was next to a rainforest. Their hospitality was amazing.\nMalaysia is a country where different cultures live side by side. I'll never forget the people who made our journey so special.","word_count":186,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS1-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"A pilot is a person ___ flies planes.","options":["which","where","who","whose"],"answer":"who","explanation":"People → who (or that).","explanation_ar":"الأشخاص ← who."},{"q":"The Hejaz Railway Museum is a place ___ you can see old trains.","options":["which","where","who","whose"],"answer":"where","explanation":"Places → where.","explanation_ar":"الأماكن ← where."},{"q":"I bought a phone case ___ has a map of the world on it.","options":["where","who","whose","which"],"answer":"which","explanation":"Things and animals → which (or that).","explanation_ar":"الأشياء ← which."},{"q":"We met a woman ___ son works at the airport.","options":["whose","which","where","who"],"answer":"whose","explanation":"Possession → whose.","explanation_ar":"الملكية ← whose."},{"q":"","options":["The tourist who lost his passport went to the embassy.","The tourist which lost his passport went to the embassy.","The tourist whose lost his passport went to the embassy.","The tourist who he lost his passport went to the embassy."],"answer":"The tourist who lost his passport went to the embassy.","explanation":"Don't repeat the pronoun after who/which.","explanation_ar":"لا تكرر الضمير."},{"q":"The cat ___ lives in our hotel garden is very friendly.","options":["who","that","whose","where"],"answer":"that","explanation":"Things and animals → which (or that).","explanation_ar":"الأشياء ← which."}]},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS1-B1","type":"gap_fill","instructions":"Guided practice: Complete with who, which, where or whose. Word bank: whose, which, where","items":[{"q":"A souvenir is something ______ you buy to remember a place.","options":[],"answer":"which","explanation":"Things and animals → which (or that).","explanation_ar":"الأشياء ← which."},{"q":"That's the restaurant ______ we had our first Malaysian meal.","options":[],"answer":"where","explanation":"Places → where.","explanation_ar":"الأماكن ← where."},{"q":"I have a classmate ______ grandfather was a sailor.","options":[],"answer":"whose","explanation":"Possession → whose.","explanation_ar":"الملكية ← whose."}]},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS1-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the mistake.","items":[{"q":"The guide which showed us the old town was very friendly.","options":[],"answer":"The guide who showed us the old town was very friendly.","explanation":"which → who. People → who (or that).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: which → who. الأشخاص ← who."},{"q":"This is the beach which we saw the turtles.","options":[],"answer":"This is the beach where we saw the turtles.","explanation":"which → where. Places → where.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: which → where. الأماكن ← where."},{"q":"I want a suitcase which it has four wheels.","options":[],"answer":"I want a suitcase which has four wheels.","explanation":"which it → which. Don't repeat the pronoun after who/which.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: which it → which. لا تكرر الضمير."}]},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS1-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Write definitions with relative clauses.","items":[{"q":"A tour guide is a person ___.","options":[],"answer":"A tour guide is a person who shows tourists around.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses defining relative clauses with who, which, that, where and whose correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"An airport is a place ___.","options":[],"answer":"An airport is a place where planes land and take off.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses defining relative clauses with who, which, that, where and whose correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A passport is a document ___.","options":[],"answer":"A passport is a document which you need to travel abroad.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses defining relative clauses with who, which, that, where and whose correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"I have a friend whose ___.","options":[],"answer":"I have a friend whose brother lives in Canada.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses defining relative clauses with who, which, that, where and whose correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"My favorite city is a place ___.","options":[],"answer":"My favorite city is a place where you can see the mountains and the sea.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses defining relative clauses with who, which, that, where and whose correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS1-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Travel quiz cards.","items":[{"q":"Write 3 quiz clues about travel words with who, which or where.","options":[],"answer":"It's a person who travels for pleasure. (tourist) It's a thing which you carry your clothes in. (suitcase) It's a place where you sleep on a trip. (hotel)","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses defining relative clauses with who, which, that, where and whose correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 sentences about a trip you took with relative clauses.","options":[],"answer":"We stayed in a hotel which had a big pool. We met a family whose children were our age.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses defining relative clauses with who, which, that, where and whose correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence about a landmark with which or that.","options":[],"answer":"The Makkah Clock Tower is a landmark that you can see from far away.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses defining relative clauses with who, which, that, where and whose correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Four Corners Grammar","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["The teacher says a sentence frame with a cue word and four possible answers (A–D).","Students walk to the corner with the correct answer.","One student in each corner explains the choice in one sentence.","Correct corner scores a point; continue with the next cue."],"data":[{"label":"a person who…"},{"label":"a place where…"},{"label":"a thing which…"},{"label":"a city that…"},{"label":"a friend whose…"},{"label":"the hotel we stayed in"},{"label":"the food I tried"},{"label":"the guide who…"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"A chef is someone ___ cooks in a restaurant.","options":["whose","where","which","who"],"answer":"who","explanation":"People → who (or that).","explanation_ar":"الأشخاص ← who."},{"q":"Riyadh is the city ___ my grandparents live.","options":["who","which","whose","where"],"answer":"where","explanation":"Places → where.","explanation_ar":"الأماكن ← where."},{"q":"This is the camera ___ I used on our trip.","options":["which","whose","where","who"],"answer":"which","explanation":"Things and animals → which (or that).","explanation_ar":"الأشياء ← which."},{"q":"Do you know anyone ___ father works for an airline?","options":["where","whose","who","which"],"answer":"whose","explanation":"Possession → whose.","explanation_ar":"الملكية ← whose."},{"q":"","options":["The bus where goes to the old town leaves at noon.","The bus that goes to the old town leaves at noon.","The bus who goes to the old town leaves at noon.","The bus that it goes to the old town leaves at noon."],"answer":"The bus that goes to the old town leaves at noon.","explanation":"Don't repeat the pronoun after who/which.","explanation_ar":"لا تكرر الضمير."},{"q":"Jeddah has many shops ___ sell traditional perfumes.","options":["who","whose","that","where"],"answer":"that","explanation":"Things and animals → which (or that).","explanation_ar":"الأشياء ← which."},{"q":"","options":["The hotel where we stayed had a rooftop garden.","The hotel which we stayed had a rooftop garden.","The hotel who we stayed had a rooftop garden.","The hotel where we stayed in it had a rooftop garden."],"answer":"The hotel where we stayed had a rooftop garden.","explanation":"Places → where.","explanation_ar":"الأماكن ← where."},{"q":"The man ___ helped us with our luggage was very kind.","options":["which","where","whose","who"],"answer":"who","explanation":"People → who (or that).","explanation_ar":"الأشخاص ← who."},{"q":"That's the student ___ essay won the travel-writing prize.","options":["which","whose","who's","who"],"answer":"whose","explanation":"Possession → whose.","explanation_ar":"الملكية ← whose."},{"q":"Which word can we use for both people and things?","options":["whose","who","that","where"],"answer":"that","explanation":"Things and animals → which (or that).","explanation_ar":"الأشياء ← which."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS1-IMG01","prompt":"Omar writing his travel blog on a tablet in a hotel lobby, with photos pinned around him: twin towers at night, a busy spice market, a guide holding a small Saudi flag, curious monkeys in a city park, and a plate of coconut rice. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"Omar writing his travel blog on a tablet in a hotel lobby, with photos pinned around him: twin towers at night, a busy spice market, a guide holding a small Saudi flag, curious monkeys in a city park, and a plate of coconut rice"},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS1-IMG02","prompt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about defining relative clauses with who, which, that, where and whose, with Zaki the Falcon pointing at a blank board on the right; leave large empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about defining relative clauses with who, which, that, where and whose, with Zaki the Falcon pointing at a blank board on the right"},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS1-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows destination (the place where someone is going); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing destination: the place where someone is going"},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS1-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows journey (traveling from one place to another, often a long way); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing journey: traveling from one place to another, often a long way"},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS1-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows landmark (a famous building or place that is easy to recognize); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing landmark: a famous building or place that is easy to recognize"},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS1-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows souvenir (something you buy to remember a place); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing souvenir: something you buy to remember a place"},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS1-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Four Corners Grammar”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Four Corners Grammar"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS1-W01","script":"destination. destination. Abha is a popular summer destination.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS1-W02","script":"journey. journey. The train journey from Riyadh to Dammam is comfortable.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS1-W03","script":"landmark. landmark. The Kingdom Centre is a Riyadh landmark.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS1-W04","script":"souvenir. souvenir. I bought a small wooden camel as a souvenir.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS1-T1","script":"Last summer, my family traveled to Kuala Lumpur, the capital of Malaysia. It's a destination that many Saudi families love. On the first day, we visited the Petronas Twin Towers, a landmark which you can see from almost everywhere in the city. We walked across the bridge that connects the two towers – it was about 170 meters above the street! Our guide was a young man who spoke Arabic very well. He took us to a market where you can find spices, colorful batik cloth and souvenirs. On the second day, we visited a museum which has beautiful models of famous mosques from around the world. In a park near the museum, monkeys that live in the trees tried to take my sister's snack! We also tried the local cuisine. My favorite dish was nasi lemak, a meal which is made with coconut rice. For two nights, we stayed with a family whose house was next to a rainforest. Their hospitality was amazing. Malaysia is a country where different cultures live side by side. I'll never forget the people who made our journey so special.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS1-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “The Guide Who Spoke Arabic: Defining Relative Clauses”. Let’s learn defining relative clauses with who, which, that, where and whose together. Listen to the start of “Omar's Travel Blog: Kuala Lumpur”: Last summer, my family traveled to Kuala Lumpur, the capital of Malaysia. It's a destination that many Saudi families love. Look at the green words. A defining relative clause gives essential information about a noun – it tells us which person, thing or place we mean: Our guide was a man who spoke Arabic. Without the clause, the sentence is not clear. Use who for people, which for things and animals, and that for people or things (very common in speaking). Use where for places (= in/at which) and whose for possession (= his/her/their): a family whose house was next to a rainforest. Here are some examples. A tourist is a person who visits a place for pleasure. The Kingdom Centre is a landmark which has a sky bridge. This is the airport where my uncle works. Be careful! Some learners say: “I met a guide which spoke Arabic.” That’s not right. We say: “I met a guide who spoke Arabic.” People → who (or that). Quick check: \"a family whose house was next to a rainforest\" – whose house? … Think! … The family's house. Great job! Now you can say: I can say \"Our guide was a man who spoke Arabic.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"0.95"}]},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS2","title":"The Souvenir I Bought: Leaving Out the Relative Pronoun","objectives":["Students will be able to tell when the relative pronoun is the subject or the object of the clause.","Students will be able to leave out who, which or that when it is the object, and put a preposition at the end of the clause.","I can say \"The souvenir I bought was cheap.\"","I can say \"That's the hotel we stayed in.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من تمييز ضمير الوصل عندما يكون فاعلًا أو مفعولًا به في جملة الصلة.","سيتمكن الطلاب من حذف who أو which أو that عندما يكون مفعولًا به، ووضع حرف الجر في نهاية جملة الصلة.","أستطيع أن أقول The souvenir I bought was cheap.","أستطيع أن أقول That's the hotel we stayed in."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"accommodation","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˌkɑməˈdeɪʃən/","definition":"a place to stay, such as a hotel or an apartment","arabic":"مكان الإقامة / السكن","example":"We booked our accommodation online.","image_id":"GV-L06-U1-LS2-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS2-W01"},{"word":"luggage","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈlʌɡədʒ/","definition":"the bags and suitcases you take on a trip","arabic":"الأمتعة","example":"Don't leave your luggage alone at the airport.","image_id":"GV-L06-U1-LS2-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS2-W02"},{"word":"cuisine","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kwɪˈzin/","definition":"a style of cooking from a country or region","arabic":"مطبخ (أسلوب الطهي)","example":"Turkish cuisine is famous for its kebabs and sweets.","image_id":"GV-L06-U1-LS2-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS2-W03"},{"word":"abroad","pos":"adverb","ipa":"/əˈbrɔd/","definition":"in or to another country","arabic":"في الخارج / إلى الخارج","example":"My cousin is studying abroad.","image_id":"GV-L06-U1-LS2-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS2-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"Look at the word after the relative pronoun. If a verb comes next, the pronoun is the subject: the guide who spoke Arabic. If a noun or pronoun comes next, the pronoun is the object: the souvenir that I bought.\nYou can leave out who/which/that when it is the object: The souvenir (that) I bought was cheap. The people (who) we met were kind. You can't leave it out when it is the subject: (not: The guide spoke Arabic was kind.)\nWith verbs + prepositions (stay in, talk to, look for), English often puts the preposition at the end: That's the hotel (which) we stayed in. (= That's the hotel where we stayed.) Don't add an extra pronoun: (not: the hotel we stayed in it).\npronoun + verb → subject → keep it\npronoun + noun/pronoun → object → you can leave it out\npreposition at the end: the man I spoke to • the city we flew to\nNot: the book I read it","examples":["The postcard my cousin sent took two weeks to arrive.","The dish we ordered was too spicy.","Is that the museum you told me about?","The people we met in Oman were very welcoming.","The guide who took us to the market spoke Arabic. (subject – can't omit)","This is the suitcase I was looking for.","The city we flew to was very cold.","Everything I packed fit in one bag."],"common_errors":["✗ The souvenir I bought it was cheap. → ✓ The souvenir I bought was cheap. (Don't add it.)","✗ The woman helped us was from Bahrain. → ✓ The woman who helped us was from Bahrain. (Subject pronoun → can't omit.)","✗ That's the hotel we stayed. → ✓ That's the hotel we stayed in. (stay in → keep the preposition.)","✗ The man to who I spoke was kind. (in normal speech) → ✓ The man I spoke to was kind. (Preposition at the end sounds natural.)"],"arabic":"إذا جاء بعد ضمير الوصل فعل فهو فاعل ولا يُحذف: the guide who spoke Arabic.\nإذا جاء بعده اسم أو ضمير فهو مفعول ويمكن حذفه: the souvenir (that) I bought.\nحرف الجر يأتي غالبًا في نهاية الجملة: the hotel we stayed in.\nلا نضيف ضميرًا زائدًا: the souvenir I bought it ✗."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “The Souvenir I Bought: Leaving Out the Relative Pronoun”. Let’s learn object vs subject relative pronouns; leaving out who/which/that when it is the object; prepositions at the end of relative clauses (the hotel we stayed in) together.","on_screen":"GV-L06-U1-LS2 | The Souvenir I Bought: Leaving Out the Relative Pronoun","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Remember these patterns: The food (that) we tried was delicious. • The girl (who) I sat next to was from Oman. The hotel (which) we booked wasn't very clean. Is this the key (that) you were looking for?","on_screen":"The food (that) we tried was delicious. • The girl (who) I sat next to was from Oman. | The hotel (which) we booked wasn't very clean. | Is this the key (that) you were looking for?","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Look at the word after the relative pronoun. If a verb comes next, the pronoun is the subject: the guide who spoke Arabic. If a noun or pronoun comes next, the pronoun is the object: the souvenir that I bought. You can leave out who/which/that when it is the object: The souvenir (that) I bought was cheap. The people (who) we met were kind. You can't leave it out when it is the subject: (not: The guide spoke Arabic was kind.)","on_screen":"pronoun + verb → subject → keep it • pronoun + noun/pronoun → object → you can leave it out • preposition at the end: the man I spoke to • the city we flew to","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The postcard my cousin sent took two weeks to arrive. The dish we ordered was too spicy. Is that the museum you told me about?","on_screen":"The postcard my cousin sent took two weeks to arrive. / The dish we ordered was too spicy. / Is that the museum you told me about?","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The souvenir I bought it was cheap.” That’s not right. We say: “The souvenir I bought was cheap.” Don't add it.","on_screen":"✗ The souvenir I bought it was cheap.   ✓ The souvenir I bought was cheap.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:05","narration":"Quick check: \"the souvenir that I bought\" – can we leave out \"that\"? … Think! … Yes – *I* comes next, so *that* is the object. Great job! Now you can say: I can say \"The souvenir I bought was cheap.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can say \"The souvenir I bought was cheap.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: object vs subject relative pronouns; leaving out who/which/that when it is the object; prepositions at the end of relative clauses (the hotel we stayed in).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","إذا جاء بعد ضمير الوصل فعل فهو فاعل ولا يُحذف: the guide who spoke Arabic.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The souvenir I bought it was cheap. — الصحيح: The souvenir I bought was cheap.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أقول The souvenir I bought was cheap."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"","options":["The souvenir liked I most was a brass lantern.","The souvenir which I liked it most was a brass lantern.","The souvenir I liked it most was a brass lantern.","The souvenir I liked most was a brass lantern."],"answer":"The souvenir I liked most was a brass lantern.","explanation":"Don't add an extra pronoun (it/him/them).","explanation_ar":"لا ضمير زائد."},{"q":"","options":["The pilot which landed the plane spoke to us.","The pilot landed the plane spoke to us.","The pilot who landed the plane spoke to us.","The pilot who he landed the plane spoke to us."],"answer":"The pilot who landed the plane spoke to us.","explanation":"Subject pronoun → you must keep it.","explanation_ar":"الفاعل ← لا يُحذف."},{"q":"In which sentence can we leave out \"that\"?","options":["The bag that has wheels is blue.","The bus that goes to the airport is late.","The bag that I bought is blue.","The plane that crashed was old."],"answer":"The bag that I bought is blue.","explanation":"Object pronoun → you can leave it out.","explanation_ar":"المفعول ← يمكن حذفه."},{"q":"That's the apartment we stayed ___ in Cairo.","options":["at it","in","in it","where"],"answer":"in","explanation":"Preposition at the end (stayed in, talked to).","explanation_ar":"حرف الجر في النهاية."},{"q":"Is this the map you were looking ___?","options":["for it","for","at","after it"],"answer":"for","explanation":"Preposition at the end (stayed in, talked to).","explanation_ar":"حرف الجر في النهاية."},{"q":"","options":["The people we met in Oman were very friendly.","The people we met them in Oman were very friendly.","The people met we in Oman were very friendly.","The people who we met them in Oman were very friendly."],"answer":"The people we met in Oman were very friendly.","explanation":"Don't add an extra pronoun (it/him/them).","explanation_ar":"لا ضمير زائد."}]},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS2-B1","type":"gap_fill","instructions":"Guided practice: Can you leave out the relative pronoun? Write Yes or No. Word bank: Yes, No","items":[{"q":"The hotel which we booked was near the beach. → ______","options":[],"answer":"Yes","explanation":"Object pronoun → you can leave it out.","explanation_ar":"المفعول ← يمكن حذفه."},{"q":"The guide who showed us the fort was from Najran. → ______","options":[],"answer":"No","explanation":"Subject pronoun → you must keep it.","explanation_ar":"الفاعل ← لا يُحذف."},{"q":"The photos that my brother took are amazing. → ______","options":[],"answer":"Yes","explanation":"Object pronoun → you can leave it out.","explanation_ar":"المفعول ← يمكن حذفه."}]},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS2-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Join the sentences. Leave out the relative pronoun.","items":[{"q":"I bought a scarf in Istanbul.  +  The scarf is red.","options":[],"answer":"The scarf I bought in Istanbul is red.","explanation":"Object pronoun → you can leave it out.","explanation_ar":"المفعول ← يمكن حذفه."},{"q":"We stayed in a hotel.  +  The hotel had a pool.","options":[],"answer":"The hotel we stayed in had a pool.","explanation":"Preposition at the end (stayed in, talked to).","explanation_ar":"حرف الجر في النهاية."},{"q":"I talked to a man at the airport.  +  He was a pilot.","options":[],"answer":"The man I talked to at the airport was a pilot.","explanation":"Preposition at the end (stayed in, talked to).","explanation_ar":"حرف الجر في النهاية."}]},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS2-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Write about your trips.","items":[{"q":"The best food I've ever tried: ___","options":[],"answer":"The best food I've ever tried was Turkish kunafa.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses object vs subject relative pronouns; leaving out who/which/that when it is the object; prepositions at the end of relative clauses (the hotel we stayed in) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A place I want to travel to: ___","options":[],"answer":"A place I want to travel to is Japan.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses object vs subject relative pronouns; leaving out who/which/that when it is the object; prepositions at the end of relative clauses (the hotel we stayed in) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"The most useful thing I packed on my last trip: ___","options":[],"answer":"The most useful thing I packed was a power bank.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses object vs subject relative pronouns; leaving out who/which/that when it is the object; prepositions at the end of relative clauses (the hotel we stayed in) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A person I met on a trip: ___","options":[],"answer":"A person I met on a trip was a guide from Egypt.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses object vs subject relative pronouns; leaving out who/which/that when it is the object; prepositions at the end of relative clauses (the hotel we stayed in) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"The city I was born in: ___","options":[],"answer":"The city I was born in is Taif.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses object vs subject relative pronouns; leaving out who/which/that when it is the object; prepositions at the end of relative clauses (the hotel we stayed in) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS2-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: My souvenir shelf.","items":[{"q":"Write 3 sentences about souvenirs you have, leaving out the relative pronoun.","options":[],"answer":"The camel I bought in Riyadh is made of wood. The shell I found in Jeddah is pink. The keychain my aunt gave me is from London.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses object vs subject relative pronouns; leaving out who/which/that when it is the object; prepositions at the end of relative clauses (the hotel we stayed in) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 sentences with a preposition at the end.","options":[],"answer":"Abha is the city I want to go back to. The hotel we stayed in had a garden.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses object vs subject relative pronouns; leaving out who/which/that when it is the object; prepositions at the end of relative clauses (the hotel we stayed in) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence where you can't leave out the pronoun, and explain why.","options":[],"answer":"The man who drove our bus was funny. – \"who\" is the subject.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses object vs subject relative pronouns; leaving out who/which/that when it is the object; prepositions at the end of relative clauses (the hotel we stayed in) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Grammar Snakes & Ladders","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["Roll and move. If you land on a cue square, make a sentence with the cue and object vs subject relative pronouns; leaving out who/which/that when it is the object; prepositions at the end of relative clauses (the hotel we stayed in).","Correct at the bottom of a ladder: climb. Incorrect at the head of a snake: slide down.","Teammates are the judges and must explain any correction.","First player to reach square 30 wins."],"data":[{"cue":"a tourist / who / visit / a new country"},{"cue":"a landmark / which / be / famous"},{"cue":"a city / where / you / can / see the sea"},{"cue":"a guide / whose / Arabic / be / excellent"},{"cue":"the souvenir / (that) / I / buy / in the market"},{"cue":"the hotel / we / stay / in"},{"cue":"a dish / which / be / made with rice"},{"cue":"the people / who / we / meet"},{"cue":"a museum / that / have / old maps"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"","options":["The dates which we bought them in Madinah were delicious.","The dates bought we in Madinah were delicious.","The dates we bought in Madinah were delicious.","The dates we bought them in Madinah were delicious."],"answer":"The dates we bought in Madinah were delicious.","explanation":"Don't add an extra pronoun (it/him/them).","explanation_ar":"لا ضمير زائد."},{"q":"","options":["The woman which sold us the tickets was very helpful.","The woman who sold us the tickets was very helpful.","The woman sold us the tickets was very helpful.","The woman who she sold us the tickets was very helpful."],"answer":"The woman who sold us the tickets was very helpful.","explanation":"Subject pronoun → you must keep it.","explanation_ar":"الفاعل ← لا يُحذف."},{"q":"In which sentence must we keep \"who\"?","options":["The boy who I sit next to is from Kuwait.","The boy who I met is from Kuwait.","The boy who you saw is from Kuwait.","The boy who sits next to me is from Kuwait."],"answer":"The boy who sits next to me is from Kuwait.","explanation":"Subject pronoun → you must keep it.","explanation_ar":"الفاعل ← لا يُحذف."},{"q":"Riyadh is the city my father grew up ___.","options":["in it","at it","where","in"],"answer":"in","explanation":"Preposition at the end (stayed in, talked to).","explanation_ar":"حرف الجر في النهاية."},{"q":"","options":["The friend with I traveled was my cousin.","The friend I traveled with was my cousin.","The friend I traveled was my cousin.","The friend I traveled with him was my cousin."],"answer":"The friend I traveled with was my cousin.","explanation":"Preposition at the end (stayed in, talked to).","explanation_ar":"حرف الجر في النهاية."},{"q":"Which word can we leave out? \"The book that I read on the plane was exciting.\"","options":["that","book","read","I"],"answer":"that","explanation":"Object pronoun → you can leave it out.","explanation_ar":"المفعول ← يمكن حذفه."},{"q":"","options":["The photos took you in Al-Ula are beautiful.","The photos you took them in Al-Ula are beautiful.","The photos you took in Al-Ula are beautiful.","The photos which you took them in Al-Ula are beautiful."],"answer":"The photos you took in Al-Ula are beautiful.","explanation":"Don't add an extra pronoun (it/him/them).","explanation_ar":"لا ضمير زائد."},{"q":"This is the bus stop I told you ___.","options":["of it","about it","where","about"],"answer":"about","explanation":"Preposition at the end (stayed in, talked to).","explanation_ar":"حرف الجر في النهاية."},{"q":"","options":["The tourists visited the museum loved it.","The tourists who they visited the museum loved it.","The tourists who visited the museum loved it.","The tourists which visited the museum loved it."],"answer":"The tourists who visited the museum loved it.","explanation":"Subject pronoun → you must keep it.","explanation_ar":"الفاعل ← لا يُحذف."},{"q":"\"The meal we had was excellent.\" What is left out?","options":["that / which","where","who","whose"],"answer":"that / which","explanation":"Object pronoun → you can leave it out.","explanation_ar":"المفعول ← يمكن حذفه."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS2-IMG01","prompt":"Lina unpacking her suitcase after a trip, laying out souvenirs on her bed with little tags: \"the scarf I bought in Istanbul\", \"the shell I found on the beach\", \"the postcard my cousin sent\". Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"Lina unpacking her suitcase after a trip, laying out souvenirs on her bed with little tags: \"the scarf I bought in Istanbul\", \"the shell I found on the beach\", \"the postcard my cousin sent\""},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS2-IMG02","prompt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of object vs subject relative pronouns; leaving out who/which/that when it is the object; prepositions at the end of relative clauses (the hotel we stayed in): a green tick panel and a soft red cross panel with simple objects from the theme “Travel and Culture”; empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of object vs subject relative pronouns"},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS2-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows accommodation (a place to stay, such as a hotel or an apartment); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing accommodation: a place to stay, such as a hotel or an apartment"},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS2-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows luggage (the bags and suitcases you take on a trip); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing luggage: the bags and suitcases you take on a trip"},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS2-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows cuisine (a style of cooking from a country or region); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing cuisine: a style of cooking from a country or region"},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS2-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows abroad (in or to another country); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing abroad: in or to another country"},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS2-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Grammar Snakes & Ladders”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Grammar Snakes & Ladders"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS2-W01","script":"accommodation. accommodation. We booked our accommodation online.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS2-W02","script":"luggage. luggage. Don't leave your luggage alone at the airport.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS2-W03","script":"cuisine. cuisine. Turkish cuisine is famous for its kebabs and sweets.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS2-W04","script":"abroad. abroad. My cousin is studying abroad.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS2-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “The Souvenir I Bought: Leaving Out the Relative Pronoun”. Let’s learn object vs subject relative pronouns; leaving out who/which/that when it is the object; prepositions at the end of relative clauses (the hotel we stayed in) together. Remember these patterns: The food (that) we tried was delicious. • The girl (who) I sat next to was from Oman. The hotel (which) we booked wasn't very clean. Is this the key (that) you were looking for? Look at the word after the relative pronoun. If a verb comes next, the pronoun is the subject: the guide who spoke Arabic. If a noun or pronoun comes next, the pronoun is the object: the souvenir that I bought. You can leave out who/which/that when it is the object: The souvenir (that) I bought was cheap. The people (who) we met were kind. You can't leave it out when it is the subject: (not: The guide spoke Arabic was kind.) Here are some examples. The postcard my cousin sent took two weeks to arrive. The dish we ordered was too spicy. Is that the museum you told me about? Be careful! Some learners say: “The souvenir I bought it was cheap.” That’s not right. We say: “The souvenir I bought was cheap.” Don't add it. Quick check: \"the souvenir that I bought\" – can we leave out \"that\"? … Think! … Yes – *I* comes next, so *that* is the object. Great job! Now you can say: I can say \"The souvenir I bought was cheap.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"0.95"}]},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS3","title":"Go Sightseeing, Catch a Flight: Travel Collocations","objectives":["Students will be able to use common travel collocations correctly.","Students will be able to choose between travel, trip and journey.","I can say \"We booked a hotel and went sightseeing.\"","I can choose \"trip\" or \"journey\" correctly."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام متلازمات السفر الشائعة بشكل صحيح.","سيتمكن الطلاب من الاختيار بين travel وtrip وjourney.","أستطيع أن أقول We booked a hotel and went sightseeing.","أستطيع أن أختار trip أو journey بشكل صحيح."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"itinerary","pos":"noun","ipa":"/aɪˈtɪnɚˌɛri/","definition":"a plan of a journey with places and times","arabic":"جدول الرحلة / خط السير","example":"Our itinerary includes three cities in five days.","image_id":"GV-L06-U1-LS3-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS3-W01"},{"word":"sightseeing","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsaɪtˈsiɪŋ/","definition":"visiting famous places as a tourist","arabic":"مشاهدة المعالم السياحية","example":"We went sightseeing in the old town.","image_id":"GV-L06-U1-LS3-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS3-W02"},{"word":"tourist","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈtʊrəst/","definition":"a person who visits a place for pleasure","arabic":"سائح","example":"Thousands of tourists visit Al-Ula every winter.","image_id":"GV-L06-U1-LS3-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS3-W03"},{"word":"local","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈloʊkəl/","definition":"from or in the area where you are","arabic":"محلّي","example":"We tried the local food at a small restaurant.","image_id":"GV-L06-U1-LS3-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS3-W04"},{"word":"explore","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɪkˈsplɔr/","definition":"to travel around a place to learn about it","arabic":"يستكشف","example":"We explored the old streets on foot.","image_id":"GV-L06-U1-LS3-W05","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS3-W05"},{"word":"hospitality","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌhɑspəˈtæləti/","definition":"being friendly and generous to guests","arabic":"حُسن الضيافة / الكرم","example":"Arab hospitality is famous around the world.","image_id":"GV-L06-U1-LS3-W06","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS3-W06"}],"explanation":{"text":"Some verbs and nouns go together naturally – these are collocations. Travel collocations: book a flight / a hotel, pack your luggage / your suitcase, check in (at the airport or hotel) / check out (of the hotel), catch / miss a flight, go sightseeing, take a trip, try the local food, buy souvenirs, explore the city.\ngo abroad – no to: We went abroad last summer. (NOT (not: went to abroad)). luggage is uncountable: two pieces of luggage (NOT (not: two luggages)).\ntravel = the general activity (noun, usually uncountable) or the verb: I love travel. We travel every summer. trip = a visit to a place and back: a school trip, a business trip. journey = moving from one place to another, often a long way: The journey took six hours.\nCollocations/phrases: book a flight / a hotel / a table • make a reservation | pack your suitcase • check in ↔ check out • catch ↔ miss a flight | go sightseeing • go abroad • take a trip • try the local food • buy souvenirs • explore the city | travel (general) • trip (there and back) • journey (from A to B)","examples":["We booked our flight three months early.","Don't forget to pack your charger.","We checked in at the hotel at 2 p.m.","Hurry, or we'll miss our flight!","In Cairo, we went sightseeing every morning.","My uncle goes abroad for work twice a year.","The school trip to the science museum was fun.","The journey from Taif to Abha takes about eight hours by car."],"common_errors":["✗ We went to abroad last year. → ✓ We went abroad last year. (No to with abroad.)","✗ I have three luggages. → ✓ I have three bags / three pieces of luggage. (luggage is uncountable.)","✗ We made sightseeing in Rome. → ✓ We went sightseeing in Rome. (go sightseeing.)","✗ How was your travel to Egypt? → ✓ How was your trip to Egypt? (A visit there and back → trip.)"],"arabic":"متلازمات السفر: book a flight (يحجز رحلة طيران) وpack your suitcase (يحزم حقيبته) وcheck in (يسجّل الوصول) وmiss a flight (تفوته الرحلة) وgo sightseeing (يتجول لمشاهدة المعالم).\nنقول go abroad دون to، وluggage غير معدودة.\ntravel = السفر بشكل عام، وtrip = زيارة وعودة، وjourney = الانتقال من مكان لآخر."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Go Sightseeing, Catch a Flight: Travel Collocations”. Let’s learn travel collocations (go sightseeing, book a flight, pack your luggage, check in, catch/miss a flight, go abroad, try local food); travel vs trip vs journey together.","on_screen":"GV-L06-U1-LS3 | Go Sightseeing, Catch a Flight: Travel Collocations","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Meet some new words. itinerary: a plan of a journey with places and times. sightseeing: visiting famous places as a tourist. tourist: a person who visits a place for pleasure.","on_screen":"itinerary | sightseeing | tourist","visual":"Each word card flips over to show its picture (vocabulary image briefs) and Arabic meaning."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Some verbs and nouns go together naturally – these are collocations. Travel collocations: book a flight / a hotel, pack your luggage / your suitcase, check in (at the airport or hotel) / check out (of the hotel), catch / miss a flight, go sightseeing, take a trip, try the local food, buy souvenirs, explore the city. go abroad – no to: We went abroad last summer. (NOT (not: went to abroad)). luggage is uncountable: two pieces of luggage (NOT (not: two luggages)).","on_screen":"travel collocations (go sightseeing, book a flight, pack your luggage, check in, catch/miss a flight, go abroad, try local food); travel vs trip vs journey","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. We booked our flight three months early. Don't forget to pack your charger. We checked in at the hotel at 2 p.m.","on_screen":"We booked our flight three months early. / Don't forget to pack your charger. / We checked in at the hotel at 2 p.m.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “We went to abroad last year.” That’s not right. We say: “We went abroad last year.” No to with abroad.","on_screen":"✗ We went to abroad last year.   ✓ We went abroad last year.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:05","narration":"Quick check: Do we say \"go to abroad\"? … Think! … No – *go abroad*. Great job! Now you can say: I can say \"We booked a hotel and went sightseeing.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can say \"We booked a hotel and went sightseeing.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: travel collocations (go sightseeing, book a flight, pack your luggage, check in, catch/miss a flight, go abroad, try local food); travel vs trip vs journey.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","متلازمات السفر: book a flight (يحجز رحلة طيران) وpack your suitcase (يحزم حقيبته) وcheck in (يسجّل الوصول) وmiss a flight (تفوته الرحلة) وgo sightseeing (يتجول لمشاهدة المعالم).","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: We went to abroad last year. — الصحيح: We went abroad last year.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أقول We booked a hotel and went sightseeing."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct word.","items":[{"q":"We ___ our hotel online last week.","options":["packed","did","booked","made"],"answer":"booked","explanation":"Choose the right verb partner (book, pack, catch, go…).","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة الصحيحة."},{"q":"The taxi was late, and we nearly ___ our flight.","options":["missed","caught up","lost","failed"],"answer":"missed","explanation":"Choose the right verb partner (book, pack, catch, go…).","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة الصحيحة."},{"q":"My cousin is studying ___ in Australia.","options":["abroad","at abroad","to abroad","in abroad"],"answer":"abroad","explanation":"go abroad (no \"to\").","explanation_ar":"go abroad."},{"q":"The ___ from Jeddah to Cairo by ship took two days.","options":["travels","travel","trip","journey"],"answer":"journey","explanation":"travel / trip / journey.","explanation_ar":"travel أو trip أو journey."},{"q":"Our class went on a ___ to the National Museum.","options":["journey","travels","travel","trip"],"answer":"trip","explanation":"travel / trip / journey.","explanation_ar":"travel أو trip أو journey."},{"q":"How much ___ are you taking?","options":["luggages","luggage","a luggage","pieces luggage"],"answer":"luggage","explanation":"luggage is uncountable.","explanation_ar":"luggage غير معدودة."}]},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS3-B1","type":"match","instructions":"Guided practice: Match the collocation halves.","items":[{"q":"go","options":["your suitcase the night before.","the early morning flight.","sightseeing in the old city","the city on foot","in at the hotel reception","the local cuisine"],"answer":"sightseeing in the old city","explanation":"go → sightseeing in the old city. Choose the right verb partner (book, pack, catch, go…).","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة الصحيحة."},{"q":"check","options":["your suitcase the night before.","the early morning flight.","sightseeing in the old city","the city on foot","in at the hotel reception","the local cuisine"],"answer":"in at the hotel reception","explanation":"check → in at the hotel reception. Choose the right verb partner (book, pack, catch, go…).","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة الصحيحة."},{"q":"try","options":["your suitcase the night before.","the early morning flight.","sightseeing in the old city","the city on foot","in at the hotel reception","the local cuisine"],"answer":"the local cuisine","explanation":"try → the local cuisine. Choose the right verb partner (book, pack, catch, go…).","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة الصحيحة."},{"q":"Don't forget to pack","options":["your suitcase the night before.","the early morning flight.","sightseeing in the old city","the city on foot","in at the hotel reception","the local cuisine"],"answer":"your suitcase the night before.","explanation":"Don't forget to pack → your suitcase the night before.. Choose the right verb partner (book, pack, catch, go…).","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة الصحيحة."},{"q":"We need to catch","options":["your suitcase the night before.","the early morning flight.","sightseeing in the old city","the city on foot","in at the hotel reception","the local cuisine"],"answer":"the early morning flight.","explanation":"We need to catch → the early morning flight.. Choose the right verb partner (book, pack, catch, go…).","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة الصحيحة."},{"q":"explore","options":["your suitcase the night before.","the early morning flight.","sightseeing in the old city","the city on foot","in at the hotel reception","the local cuisine"],"answer":"the city on foot","explanation":"explore → the city on foot. Choose the right verb partner (book, pack, catch, go…).","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة الصحيحة."}]},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS3-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Talk about your travels.","items":[{"q":"A place where you went sightseeing: ___","options":[],"answer":"I went sightseeing in Istanbul.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses travel collocations (go sightseeing, book a flight, pack your luggage, check in, catch/miss a flight, go abroad, try local food); travel vs trip vs journey correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Something you always pack: ___","options":[],"answer":"I always pack a book and a charger.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses travel collocations (go sightseeing, book a flight, pack your luggage, check in, catch/miss a flight, go abroad, try local food); travel vs trip vs journey correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A local dish you tried on a trip: ___","options":[],"answer":"I tried mandi in Yemen-style restaurants in Jeddah.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses travel collocations (go sightseeing, book a flight, pack your luggage, check in, catch/miss a flight, go abroad, try local food); travel vs trip vs journey correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Your longest journey: ___","options":[],"answer":"My longest journey was a twelve-hour flight to Malaysia.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses travel collocations (go sightseeing, book a flight, pack your luggage, check in, catch/miss a flight, go abroad, try local food); travel vs trip vs journey correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A trip you want to take one day: ___","options":[],"answer":"I want to take a trip to the Maldives one day.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses travel collocations (go sightseeing, book a flight, pack your luggage, check in, catch/miss a flight, go abroad, try local food); travel vs trip vs journey correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS3-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: My dream trip.","items":[{"q":"Write 3 sentences about a dream trip using 3 collocations.","options":[],"answer":"I'll book a flight to Japan. I'll go sightseeing in Tokyo. I'll try the local food.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses travel collocations (go sightseeing, book a flight, pack your luggage, check in, catch/miss a flight, go abroad, try local food); travel vs trip vs journey correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 sentences: one with trip and one with journey.","options":[],"answer":"Our trip to Abha was amazing. The journey was long but beautiful.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses travel collocations (go sightseeing, book a flight, pack your luggage, check in, catch/miss a flight, go abroad, try local food); travel vs trip vs journey correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one piece of advice for someone who is going abroad.","options":[],"answer":"Before you go abroad, check that your passport is valid.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses travel collocations (go sightseeing, book a flight, pack your luggage, check in, catch/miss a flight, go abroad, try local food); travel vs trip vs journey correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Word Dominoes","mechanic":"matching","rules":["Deal the dominoes equally in teams of four.","Player 1 places a domino; the next player adds a domino whose word matches the open definition.","If you cannot play, pass.","The first player with no dominoes left wins."],"data":[{"word":"destination","match":"the place where someone is going","arabic":"وِجهة"},{"word":"journey","match":"traveling from one place to another, often a long way","arabic":"رحلة (انتقال من مكان لآخر)"},{"word":"landmark","match":"a famous building or place that is easy to recognize","arabic":"معلم بارز"},{"word":"souvenir","match":"something you buy to remember a place","arabic":"تذكار"},{"word":"accommodation","match":"a place to stay, such as a hotel or an apartment","arabic":"مكان الإقامة / السكن"},{"word":"luggage","match":"the bags and suitcases you take on a trip","arabic":"الأمتعة"},{"word":"cuisine","match":"a style of cooking from a country or region","arabic":"مطبخ (أسلوب الطهي)"},{"word":"abroad","match":"in or to another country","arabic":"في الخارج / إلى الخارج"},{"word":"itinerary","match":"a plan of a journey with places and times","arabic":"جدول الرحلة / خط السير"},{"word":"sightseeing","match":"visiting famous places as a tourist","arabic":"مشاهدة المعالم السياحية"},{"word":"tourist","match":"a person who visits a place for pleasure","arabic":"سائح"},{"word":"local","match":"from or in the area where you are","arabic":"محلّي"},{"word":"explore","match":"to travel around a place to learn about it","arabic":"يستكشف"},{"word":"hospitality","match":"being friendly and generous to guests","arabic":"حُسن الضيافة / الكرم"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"We ___ out of the hotel at 11 a.m.","options":["packed","caught","booked","checked"],"answer":"checked","explanation":"Choose the right verb partner (book, pack, catch, go…).","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة الصحيحة."},{"q":"Let's ___ the local food tonight.","options":["try","do","make","catch"],"answer":"try","explanation":"Choose the right verb partner (book, pack, catch, go…).","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة الصحيحة."},{"q":"My father ___ the 6 a.m. flight to Dammam.","options":["took on","got on to","caught","reached"],"answer":"caught","explanation":"Choose the right verb partner (book, pack, catch, go…).","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة الصحيحة."},{"q":"","options":["Have you ever been in abroad?","Have you ever been at abroad?","Have you ever been to abroad?","Have you ever been abroad?"],"answer":"Have you ever been abroad?","explanation":"go abroad (no \"to\").","explanation_ar":"go abroad."},{"q":"The ___ to the airport took only twenty minutes.","options":["travels","travel","journey","trips"],"answer":"journey","explanation":"travel / trip / journey.","explanation_ar":"travel أو trip أو journey."},{"q":"How was your ___ to Bahrain? Did you enjoy it?","options":["journeying","trip","travels","travel"],"answer":"trip","explanation":"travel / trip / journey.","explanation_ar":"travel أو trip أو journey."},{"q":"","options":["We had two luggage.","We had two luggages.","We had two pieces of luggage.","We had two pieces of luggages."],"answer":"We had two pieces of luggage.","explanation":"luggage is uncountable.","explanation_ar":"luggage غير معدودة."},{"q":"A plan of a journey with places and times is an ___.","options":["itinerary","souvenir","accommodation","luggage"],"answer":"itinerary","explanation":"Choose the travel word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."},{"q":"Arab ___ means being generous and kind to guests.","options":["cuisine","luggage","itinerary","hospitality"],"answer":"hospitality","explanation":"Choose the travel word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."},{"q":"Which collocation is natural?","options":["do sightseeing","go sightseeing","make sightseeing","take sightseeing"],"answer":"go sightseeing","explanation":"Choose the right verb partner (book, pack, catch, go…).","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة الصحيحة."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS3-IMG01","prompt":"An airport check-in hall: a family packing and weighing luggage, a departures board showing flights, a girl buying souvenirs at a shop, and Zaki the Falcon wearing a backpack and holding an itinerary. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"An airport check-in hall: a family packing and weighing luggage, a departures board showing flights, a girl buying souvenirs at a shop, and Zaki the Falcon wearing a backpack and holding an itinerary"},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS3-IMG02","prompt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 6 small picture icons of the lesson words; empty labels. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 6 small picture icons of the lesson words"},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS3-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows itinerary (a plan of a journey with places and times); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing itinerary: a plan of a journey with places and times"},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS3-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows sightseeing (visiting famous places as a tourist); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing sightseeing: visiting famous places as a tourist"},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS3-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows tourist (a person who visits a place for pleasure); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing tourist: a person who visits a place for pleasure"},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS3-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows local (from or in the area where you are); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing local: from or in the area where you are"},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS3-W05","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows explore (to travel around a place to learn about it); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing explore: to travel around a place to learn about it"},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS3-W06","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows hospitality (being friendly and generous to guests); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing hospitality: being friendly and generous to guests"},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS3-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Word Dominoes”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Word Dominoes"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS3-W01","script":"itinerary. itinerary. Our itinerary includes three cities in five days.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS3-W02","script":"sightseeing. sightseeing. We went sightseeing in the old town.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS3-W03","script":"tourist. tourist. Thousands of tourists visit Al-Ula every winter.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS3-W04","script":"local. local. We tried the local food at a small restaurant.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS3-W05","script":"explore. explore. We explored the old streets on foot.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS3-W06","script":"hospitality. hospitality. Arab hospitality is famous around the world.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS3-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Go Sightseeing, Catch a Flight: Travel Collocations”. Let’s learn travel collocations (go sightseeing, book a flight, pack your luggage, check in, catch/miss a flight, go abroad, try local food); travel vs trip vs journey together. Meet some new words. itinerary: a plan of a journey with places and times. sightseeing: visiting famous places as a tourist. tourist: a person who visits a place for pleasure. Some verbs and nouns go together naturally – these are collocations. Travel collocations: book a flight / a hotel, pack your luggage / your suitcase, check in (at the airport or hotel) / check out (of the hotel), catch / miss a flight, go sightseeing, take a trip, try the local food, buy souvenirs, explore the city. go abroad – no to: We went abroad last summer. (NOT (not: went to abroad)). luggage is uncountable: two pieces of luggage (NOT (not: two luggages)). Here are some examples. We booked our flight three months early. Don't forget to pack your charger. We checked in at the hotel at 2 p.m. Be careful! Some learners say: “We went to abroad last year.” That’s not right. We say: “We went abroad last year.” No to with abroad. Quick check: Do we say \"go to abroad\"? … Think! … No – *go abroad*. Great job! Now you can say: I can say \"We booked a hotel and went sightseeing.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"0.95"}]},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS4","title":"I Met a Guide. He Spoke Arabic. → Combining Sentences with Relative Clauses","objectives":["Students will be able to combine two short sentences into one with a defining relative clause.","Students will be able to avoid repeated pronouns and choose the correct relative word.","I can join \"I met a guide. He spoke Arabic.\" into one sentence.","I can make my writing smoother with relative clauses."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من دمج جملتين قصيرتين في جملة واحدة بجملة صلة محدِّدة.","سيتمكن الطلاب من تجنّب تكرار الضمائر واختيار كلمة الوصل الصحيحة.","أستطيع أن أدمج I met a guide. He spoke Arabic. في جملة واحدة.","أستطيع أن أجعل كتابتي أكثر سلاسة بجمل الصلة."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"destination","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌdɛstəˈneɪʃən/","definition":"the place where someone is going","arabic":"وِجهة","example":"Abha is a popular summer destination.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"journey","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈdʒɝni/","definition":"traveling from one place to another, often a long way","arabic":"رحلة (انتقال من مكان لآخر)","example":"The train journey from Riyadh to Dammam is comfortable.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"landmark","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈlændˌmɑrk/","definition":"a famous building or place that is easy to recognize","arabic":"معلم بارز","example":"The Kingdom Centre is a Riyadh landmark.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"souvenir","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌsuvəˈnɪr/","definition":"something you buy to remember a place","arabic":"تذكار","example":"I bought a small wooden camel as a souvenir.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"accommodation","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˌkɑməˈdeɪʃən/","definition":"a place to stay, such as a hotel or an apartment","arabic":"مكان الإقامة / السكن","example":"We booked our accommodation online.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"luggage","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈlʌɡədʒ/","definition":"the bags and suitcases you take on a trip","arabic":"الأمتعة","example":"Don't leave your luggage alone at the airport.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"Too many short sentences sound choppy: I met a guide. He spoke Arabic. He was from Morocco. You can combine them with a relative clause: I met a guide who spoke Arabic.\nSteps: 1) find the same person, thing or place in both sentences; 2) replace the second one with who / which / that / where / whose; 3) put the clause right after the noun it describes; 4) delete the repeated pronoun (he, it, there, his).\nFor places, there becomes where: We visited a village. People make pottery there. → We visited a village where people make pottery. For possession, his / her / its / their becomes whose: I have a friend. His father is a pilot. → I have a friend whose father is a pilot.\nsame noun in both sentences → relative word\nhe/she/they → who • it → which/that • there → where • his/her/their → whose\nput the clause right after the noun\ndelete the repeated pronoun","examples":["We visited a souq. It sells spices. → We visited a souq which sells spices.","I met a girl. She speaks Malay. → I met a girl who speaks Malay.","This is the café. We had breakfast there. → This is the café where we had breakfast.","I know a boy. His mother is a travel agent. → I know a boy whose mother is a travel agent.","The plane was late. It was going to Riyadh. → The plane that was going to Riyadh was late.","The museum was closed. We wanted to visit it. → The museum we wanted to visit was closed.","I loved the hotel. It had a view of the sea. → I loved the hotel which had a view of the sea.","Abha is a city. It's cool in summer. → Abha is a city that is cool in summer."],"common_errors":["✗ We visited a village where people make pottery there. → ✓ We visited a village where people make pottery. (Delete there.)","✗ I have a friend who his father is a pilot. → ✓ I have a friend whose father is a pilot. (who his → whose.)","✗ The plane was late which was going to Riyadh. → ✓ The plane which was going to Riyadh was late. (Put the clause right after the noun.)","✗ I loved the hotel which it had a view of the sea. → ✓ I loved the hotel which had a view of the sea. (Delete it.)"],"arabic":"الجمل القصيرة الكثيرة تبدو متقطعة، فندمجها بجملة صلة: I met a guide who spoke Arabic.\nالخطوات: نجد الاسم المشترك، ونستبدل الثاني بـ who/which/where/whose، ونضع الجملة بعد الاسم مباشرة، ونحذف الضمير المكرر.\nthere تصبح where، وhis/her/their تصبح whose."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “I Met a Guide. He Spoke Arabic. → Combining Sentences with Relative Clauses”. Let’s learn combining two sentences with who, which, that, where and whose; avoiding repeated pronouns; sentence variety together.","on_screen":"GV-L06-U1-LS4 | I Met a Guide. He Spoke Arabic. → Combining Sentences with Relative Clauses","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Too many short sentences sound choppy: I met a guide. He spoke Arabic. He was from Morocco. You can combine them with a relative clause: I met a guide who spoke Arabic.","on_screen":"combining two sentences with who, which, that, where and whose; avoiding repeated pronouns; sentence variety","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Too many short sentences sound choppy: I met a guide. He spoke Arabic. He was from Morocco. You can combine them with a relative clause: I met a guide who spoke Arabic. Steps: 1) find the same person, thing or place in both sentences; 2) replace the second one with who / which / that / where / whose; 3) put the clause right after the noun it describes; 4) delete the repeated pronoun (he, it, there, his).","on_screen":"same noun in both sentences → relative word • he/she/they → who • it → which/that • there → where • his/her/their → whose • put the clause right after the noun","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. We visited a souq. It sells spices. → We visited a souq which sells spices. I met a girl. She speaks Malay. → I met a girl who speaks Malay. This is the café. We had breakfast there. → This is the café where we had breakfast.","on_screen":"We visited a souq. It sells spices. → We visited a souq which sells spices. / I met a girl. She speaks Malay. → I met a girl who speaks Malay. / This is the café. We had breakfast there. → This is the café where we had breakfast.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “We visited a village where people make pottery there.” That’s not right. We say: “We visited a village where people make pottery.” Delete there.","on_screen":"✗ We visited a village where people make pottery there.   ✓ We visited a village where people make pottery.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:05","narration":"Quick check: \"I have a friend. His father is a pilot.\" Which word replaces \"His\"? … Think! … *whose*. Great job! Now you can say: I can join \"I met a guide. He spoke Arabic.\" into one sentence.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can join \"I met a guide. He spoke Arabic.\" into one sentence.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: combining two sentences with who, which, that, where and whose; avoiding repeated pronouns; sentence variety.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","الجمل القصيرة الكثيرة تبدو متقطعة، فندمجها بجملة صلة: I met a guide who spoke Arabic.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: We visited a village where people make pottery there. — الصحيح: We visited a village where people make pottery.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أدمج I met a guide. He spoke Arabic. في جملة واحدة."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"My Trip to Egypt (editing passage)","body":"Last spring, we visited Cairo, a city where it has thousands of years of history. Our guide was a woman which knew everything about the pyramids. We visited a museum where we saw a gold mask there. We also met a man who his shop sold papyrus paintings. The hotel which we stayed had a view of the Nile. I bought a souvenir which it was a small model pyramid.","word_count":70,"audio_id":""}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the best combined sentence.","items":[{"q":"","options":["We stayed in a hotel which it had a rooftop pool.","We stayed in a hotel where had a rooftop pool.","We stayed in a hotel which had a rooftop pool.","We stayed in a hotel who had a rooftop pool."],"answer":"We stayed in a hotel which had a rooftop pool.","explanation":"it (things) → which / that.","explanation_ar":"للأشياء which."},{"q":"","options":["I met a pilot which has flown to forty countries.","I met a pilot who has flown to forty countries.","I met a pilot whose has flown to forty countries.","I met a pilot who he has flown to forty countries."],"answer":"I met a pilot who has flown to forty countries.","explanation":"he/she/they (people) → who.","explanation_ar":"للأشخاص who."},{"q":"","options":["That's the beach where we saw the dolphins.","That's the beach who we saw the dolphins.","That's the beach which we saw the dolphins.","That's the beach where we saw the dolphins there."],"answer":"That's the beach where we saw the dolphins.","explanation":"there (places) → where.","explanation_ar":"there ← where."},{"q":"","options":["I have a cousin who's house is next to the Red Sea.","I have a cousin who his house is next to the Red Sea.","I have a cousin which house is next to the Red Sea.","I have a cousin whose house is next to the Red Sea."],"answer":"I have a cousin whose house is next to the Red Sea.","explanation":"his/her/their → whose.","explanation_ar":"الملكية ← whose."},{"q":"","options":["The train that it goes to Madinah is very fast.","The train is very fast who goes to Madinah.","The train is very fast that goes to Madinah.","The train that goes to Madinah is very fast."],"answer":"The train that goes to Madinah is very fast.","explanation":"Put the clause right after the noun and delete the repeated pronoun.","explanation_ar":"مكان الجملة وحذف الضمير."},{"q":"How can we combine \"We visited a farm. They grow roses there.\"?","options":["We visited a farm where they grow roses there.","We visited a farm who they grow roses.","We visited a farm where they grow roses.","We visited a farm which they grow roses."],"answer":"We visited a farm where they grow roses.","explanation":"there (places) → where.","explanation_ar":"there ← where."}]},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS4-B1","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Combine the sentences.","items":[{"q":"I bought a lantern.  +  It was made of brass.","options":[],"answer":"I bought a lantern which was made of brass.","explanation":"it (things) → which / that.","explanation_ar":"للأشياء which."},{"q":"We met a family.  +  They lived near the rainforest.","options":[],"answer":"We met a family who lived near the rainforest.","explanation":"he/she/they (people) → who.","explanation_ar":"للأشخاص who."},{"q":"We ate at a restaurant.  +  The chef was from Lebanon. (the restaurant's chef)","options":[],"answer":"We ate at a restaurant whose chef was from Lebanon.","explanation":"his/her/their → whose.","explanation_ar":"الملكية ← whose."}]},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS4-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Combine the sentences. Use where.","items":[{"q":"This is the market.  +  My grandmother buys fabric there.","options":[],"answer":"This is the market where my grandmother buys fabric.","explanation":"there (places) → where.","explanation_ar":"there ← where."},{"q":"Taif is the city.  +  I was born there.","options":[],"answer":"Taif is the city where I was born.","explanation":"there (places) → where.","explanation_ar":"there ← where."},{"q":"We found a café.  +  We could see the mountains from there.","options":[],"answer":"We found a café where we could see the mountains.","explanation":"there (places) → where.","explanation_ar":"there ← where."}]},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS4-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Edit the travel diary.","items":[{"q":"Last spring, we visited Cairo, a city where it has thousands of years of history.","options":[],"answer":"Last spring, we visited Cairo, a city which has thousands of years of history.","explanation":"where it → which. he/she/they (people) → who.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: where it → which. للأشخاص who."},{"q":"Our guide was a woman which knew everything about the pyramids.","options":[],"answer":"Our guide was a woman who knew everything about the pyramids.","explanation":"which → who. he/she/they (people) → who.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: which → who. للأشخاص who."},{"q":"We visited a museum where we saw a gold mask there.","options":[],"answer":"We visited a museum where we saw a gold mask.","explanation":"where we saw a gold mask there → where we saw a gold mask. he/she/they (people) → who.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: where we saw a gold mask there → where we saw a gold mask. للأشخاص who."},{"q":"We also met a man who his shop sold papyrus paintings.","options":[],"answer":"We also met a man whose shop sold papyrus paintings.","explanation":"who his → whose. he/she/they (people) → who.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: who his → whose. للأشخاص who."},{"q":"The hotel which we stayed had a view of the Nile.","options":[],"answer":"The hotel where we stayed had a view of the Nile.","explanation":"which we stayed → where we stayed. he/she/they (people) → who.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: which we stayed → where we stayed. للأشخاص who."},{"q":"I bought a souvenir which it was a small model pyramid.","options":[],"answer":"I bought a souvenir which was a small model pyramid.","explanation":"which it → which. he/she/they (people) → who.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: which it → which. للأشخاص who."}]},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS4-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Make your writing smoother.","items":[{"q":"Combine: \"I have a friend. She has visited ten countries.\"","options":[],"answer":"I have a friend who has visited ten countries.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses combining two sentences with who, which, that, where and whose; avoiding repeated pronouns; sentence variety correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Combine: \"We went to a park. Children can ride camels there.\"","options":[],"answer":"We went to a park where children can ride camels.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses combining two sentences with who, which, that, where and whose; avoiding repeated pronouns; sentence variety correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 3 short sentences about a trip, then combine two of them with a relative clause.","options":[],"answer":"We went to Abha. We stayed in a cabin. It was in the forest. → We stayed in a cabin which was in the forest.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses combining two sentences with who, which, that, where and whose; avoiding repeated pronouns; sentence variety correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Speed Spell","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["The teacher says a word and a short sentence with it.","Students write the word in 10 seconds.","Show boards on \"Show me!\". Correct = 1 point.","After 10 words, partners check each other's boards and correct any mistakes."],"data":[{"word":"destination","clue":"the place where someone is going"},{"word":"accommodation","clue":"a place to stay, such as a hotel or an apartment"},{"word":"itinerary","clue":"a plan of a journey with places and times"},{"word":"souvenir","clue":"something you buy to remember a place"},{"word":"luggage","clue":"the bags and suitcases you take on a trip"},{"word":"cuisine","clue":"a style of cooking from a country or region"},{"word":"journey","clue":"traveling from one place to another, often a long way"},{"word":"landmark","clue":"a famous building or place that is easy to recognize"},{"word":"abroad","clue":"in or to another country"},{"word":"sightseeing","clue":"visiting famous places as a tourist"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"How can we combine \"I know a girl. Her brother is a tour guide.\"?","options":["I know a girl whose brother is a tour guide.","I know a girl which brother is a tour guide.","I know a girl who's brother is a tour guide.","I know a girl who her brother is a tour guide."],"answer":"I know a girl whose brother is a tour guide.","explanation":"his/her/their → whose.","explanation_ar":"الملكية ← whose."},{"q":"","options":["The souq which we bought the spices was very busy.","The souq where we bought the spices there was very busy.","The souq where we bought the spices was very busy.","The souq who we bought the spices was very busy."],"answer":"The souq where we bought the spices was very busy.","explanation":"there (places) → where.","explanation_ar":"there ← where."},{"q":"","options":["The boat who took us to the island was small.","The boat which it took us to the island was small.","The boat was small which took us to the island.","The boat which took us to the island was small."],"answer":"The boat which took us to the island was small.","explanation":"it (things) → which / that.","explanation_ar":"للأشياء which."},{"q":"Which word replaces \"there\" when we combine sentences?","options":["who","whose","which","where"],"answer":"where","explanation":"there (places) → where.","explanation_ar":"there ← where."},{"q":"","options":["The tourists which visited the farm loved the strawberries.","The tourists loved the strawberries who visited the farm.","The tourists who visited the farm loved the strawberries.","The tourists who they visited the farm loved the strawberries."],"answer":"The tourists who visited the farm loved the strawberries.","explanation":"he/she/they (people) → who.","explanation_ar":"للأشخاص who."},{"q":"Which word replaces \"his\" when we combine sentences?","options":["whose","where","which","who"],"answer":"whose","explanation":"his/her/their → whose.","explanation_ar":"الملكية ← whose."},{"q":"","options":["We visited a town where is famous for its honey.","We visited a town that it is famous for its honey.","We visited a town who is famous for its honey.","We visited a town that is famous for its honey."],"answer":"We visited a town that is famous for its honey.","explanation":"it (things) → which / that.","explanation_ar":"للأشياء which."},{"q":"What is wrong with \"This is the hotel where we stayed there.\"?","options":["Nothing is wrong.","It needs \"which\".","\"there\" is repeated.","It needs a comma."],"answer":"\"there\" is repeated.","explanation":"Put the clause right after the noun and delete the repeated pronoun.","explanation_ar":"مكان الجملة وحذف الضمير."},{"q":"","options":["The man who his car broke down called for help.","The man whose car broke down called for help.","The man which car broke down called for help.","The man who's car broke down called for help."],"answer":"The man whose car broke down called for help.","explanation":"his/her/their → whose.","explanation_ar":"الملكية ← whose."},{"q":"How can we combine \"The plane was full. We wanted to take it.\"?","options":["The plane was full we wanted to take.","The plane we wanted to take was full.","The plane we wanted to take it was full.","The plane who we wanted to take was full."],"answer":"The plane we wanted to take was full.","explanation":"Put the clause right after the noun and delete the repeated pronoun.","explanation_ar":"مكان الجملة وحذف الضمير."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS4-IMG01","prompt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Travel and Culture” that shows combining two sentences with who, which, that, where and whose; avoiding repeated pronouns; sentence variety in action. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Travel and Culture” that shows combining two sentences with who, which, that, where and whose; avoiding repeated pronouns; sentence variety in action"},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS4-IMG02","prompt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen"},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS4-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Speed Spell”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Speed Spell"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS4-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “I Met a Guide. He Spoke Arabic. → Combining Sentences with Relative Clauses”. Let’s learn combining two sentences with who, which, that, where and whose; avoiding repeated pronouns; sentence variety together. Too many short sentences sound choppy: I met a guide. He spoke Arabic. He was from Morocco. You can combine them with a relative clause: I met a guide who spoke Arabic. Too many short sentences sound choppy: I met a guide. He spoke Arabic. He was from Morocco. You can combine them with a relative clause: I met a guide who spoke Arabic. Steps: 1) find the same person, thing or place in both sentences; 2) replace the second one with who / which / that / where / whose; 3) put the clause right after the noun it describes; 4) delete the repeated pronoun (he, it, there, his). Here are some examples. We visited a souq. It sells spices. → We visited a souq which sells spices. I met a girl. She speaks Malay. → I met a girl who speaks Malay. This is the café. We had breakfast there. → This is the café where we had breakfast. Be careful! Some learners say: “We visited a village where people make pottery there.” That’s not right. We say: “We visited a village where people make pottery.” Delete there. Quick check: \"I have a friend. His father is a pilot.\" Which word replaces \"His\"? … Think! … *whose*. Great job! Now you can say: I can join \"I met a guide. He spoke Arabic.\" into one sentence.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"0.95"}]},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS5","title":"A Visitor's Guide to My City: Language in Use","objectives":["Students will be able to describe places, people and things in their city with relative clauses.","Students will be able to write a short visitor's guide with travel collocations and smooth, combined sentences.","I can recommend places in my city to a visitor.","I can write a short visitor's guide."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من وصف الأماكن والأشخاص والأشياء في مدينتهم بجمل الصلة.","سيتمكن الطلاب من كتابة دليل زائر قصير بمتلازمات السفر وجمل مدمجة سلسة.","أستطيع أن أنصح الزائر بأماكن في مدينتي.","أستطيع أن أكتب دليل زائر قصيرًا."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"destination","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌdɛstəˈneɪʃən/","definition":"the place where someone is going","arabic":"وِجهة","example":"Abha is a popular summer destination.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"journey","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈdʒɝni/","definition":"traveling from one place to another, often a long way","arabic":"رحلة (انتقال من مكان لآخر)","example":"The train journey from Riyadh to Dammam is comfortable.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"landmark","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈlændˌmɑrk/","definition":"a famous building or place that is easy to recognize","arabic":"معلم بارز","example":"The Kingdom Centre is a Riyadh landmark.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"souvenir","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌsuvəˈnɪr/","definition":"something you buy to remember a place","arabic":"تذكار","example":"I bought a small wooden camel as a souvenir.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"accommodation","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˌkɑməˈdeɪʃən/","definition":"a place to stay, such as a hotel or an apartment","arabic":"مكان الإقامة / السكن","example":"We booked our accommodation online.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"luggage","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈlʌɡədʒ/","definition":"the bags and suitcases you take on a trip","arabic":"الأمتعة","example":"Don't leave your luggage alone at the airport.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"cuisine","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kwɪˈzin/","definition":"a style of cooking from a country or region","arabic":"مطبخ (أسلوب الطهي)","example":"Turkish cuisine is famous for its kebabs and sweets.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"abroad","pos":"adverb","ipa":"/əˈbrɔd/","definition":"in or to another country","arabic":"في الخارج / إلى الخارج","example":"My cousin is studying abroad.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"itinerary","pos":"noun","ipa":"/aɪˈtɪnɚˌɛri/","definition":"a plan of a journey with places and times","arabic":"جدول الرحلة / خط السير","example":"Our itinerary includes three cities in five days.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"sightseeing","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsaɪtˈsiɪŋ/","definition":"visiting famous places as a tourist","arabic":"مشاهدة المعالم السياحية","example":"We went sightseeing in the old town.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"tourist","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈtʊrəst/","definition":"a person who visits a place for pleasure","arabic":"سائح","example":"Thousands of tourists visit Al-Ula every winter.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"local","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈloʊkəl/","definition":"from or in the area where you are","arabic":"محلّي","example":"We tried the local food at a small restaurant.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"explore","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɪkˈsplɔr/","definition":"to travel around a place to learn about it","arabic":"يستكشف","example":"We explored the old streets on foot.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"hospitality","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌhɑspəˈtæləti/","definition":"being friendly and generous to guests","arabic":"حُسن الضيافة / الكرم","example":"Arab hospitality is famous around the world.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"Today you use all of Unit 1: defining relative clauses, leaving out the relative pronoun, travel collocations and combining sentences.\nA visitor's guide recommends places (visit…, take…, try…), describes them (a city where…, a palace that…) and gives tips.\n✔ who / which / that / where / whose\n✔ object pronoun can be left out: the honey they make\n✔ go sightseeing, try the local food, buy souvenirs, book a hotel\n✔ combine short sentences – no repeated pronouns","examples":["Al-Baha is a region where you can walk through juniper forests.","Visit the market which opens every Friday.","The guide who works at the fort is very friendly.","The dates they sell here are the best in the region.","Book your hotel early in the holidays.","Go sightseeing in the old town in the evening.","Meet the artist whose paintings are in the gallery.","Don't forget to try the local coffee."],"common_errors":["✗ Taif is a city which the mountains meet the clouds. → ✓ Taif is a city where the mountains meet the clouds. (Place → where.)","✗ The rose water they produce it is famous. → ✓ The rose water they produce is famous. (Don't add it.)","✗ You can make sightseeing at the palace. → ✓ You can go sightseeing at the palace. (go sightseeing.)"],"arabic":"نراجع جمل الصلة المحدِّدة وحذف ضمير الوصل ومتلازمات السفر ودمج الجمل.\nدليل الزائر يقترح الأماكن ويصفها ويقدّم نصائح.\nتذكّر: where للمكان، وwhose للملكية، ولا تكرّر الضمير."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “A Visitor's Guide to My City: Language in Use”. Let’s learn defining relative clauses, omitted relative pronouns, travel collocations, sentence combining (unit review) together.","on_screen":"GV-L06-U1-LS5 | A Visitor's Guide to My City: Language in Use","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Tourist information desk: Student A is a tourist visiting your city; Student B works at the information desk. The tourist asks 4 questions (Where can I…? What's a place where…?). The desk worker answers with relative clauses and travel collocations. Then swap.","on_screen":"Language in use","visual":"Split screen: two cast members talking, with speech bubbles appearing."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Today you use all of Unit 1: defining relative clauses, leaving out the relative pronoun, travel collocations and combining sentences. A visitor's guide recommends places (visit…, take…, try…), describes them (a city where…, a palace that…) and gives tips.","on_screen":"✔ who / which / that / where / whose • ✔ object pronoun can be left out: the honey they make • ✔ go sightseeing, try the local food, buy souvenirs, book a hotel","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Al-Baha is a region where you can walk through juniper forests. Visit the market which opens every Friday. The guide who works at the fort is very friendly.","on_screen":"Al-Baha is a region where you can walk through juniper forests. / Visit the market which opens every Friday. / The guide who works at the fort is very friendly.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Taif is a city which the mountains meet the clouds.” That’s not right. We say: “Taif is a city where the mountains meet the clouds.” Place → where.","on_screen":"✗ Taif is a city which the mountains meet the clouds.   ✓ Taif is a city where the mountains meet the clouds.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:05","narration":"Quick check: what is wrong with “The rose water they produce it is famous.”? … Think! … It should be “The rose water they produce is famous.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can recommend places in my city to a visitor.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can recommend places in my city to a visitor.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: defining relative clauses, omitted relative pronouns, travel collocations, sentence combining (unit review).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","نراجع جمل الصلة المحدِّدة وحذف ضمير الوصل ومتلازمات السفر ودمج الجمل.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Taif is a city which the mountains meet the clouds. — الصحيح: Taif is a city where the mountains meet the clouds.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أنصح الزائر بأماكن في مدينتي."]},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Welcome to Taif! – A Visitor's Guide by Grade 8","body":"Taif is a city where the mountains meet the clouds.\nIt's a perfect destination for tourists who want cool weather in summer.\nFirst, visit the rose farms which make Taif famous. The rose water they produce is sold all over the world.\nNext, take the cable car that goes down from Al-Hada through the mountains – the view is amazing!\nThen go sightseeing at Shubra Palace, a building that is more than a hundred years old.\nIn the souq, you can buy souvenirs and try the local honey.\nBefore you leave, have lunch with a family whose hospitality you will never forget.","word_count":102,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS5-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS5-A1","type":"true_false","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the visitor's guide. True or False?","items":[{"q":"Taif is a good destination for tourists who want cool summer weather.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Welcome to Taif! – A Visitor's Guide by Grade 8”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"The rose water from Taif is only sold in Saudi Arabia.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Welcome to Taif! – A Visitor's Guide by Grade 8”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"The cable car goes down from Al-Hada through the mountains.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Welcome to Taif! – A Visitor's Guide by Grade 8”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Shubra Palace is a new building.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Welcome to Taif! – A Visitor's Guide by Grade 8”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Visitors can try local honey in the souq.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Welcome to Taif! – A Visitor's Guide by Grade 8”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"The guide tells visitors to have lunch at a big hotel.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Welcome to Taif! – A Visitor's Guide by Grade 8”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."}]},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS5-B1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Guided practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"Taif is a city ___ the mountains meet the clouds.","options":["who","which","where","whose"],"answer":"where","explanation":"Relative clause (who / which / that / where / whose).","explanation_ar":"جملة الصلة."},{"q":"It's perfect for tourists ___ want cool weather.","options":["who","which","whose","where"],"answer":"who","explanation":"Relative clause (who / which / that / where / whose).","explanation_ar":"جملة الصلة."},{"q":"","options":["The rose water produce they is sold all over the world.","The rose water which they produce it is sold all over the world.","The rose water they produce it is sold all over the world.","The rose water they produce is sold all over the world."],"answer":"The rose water they produce is sold all over the world.","explanation":"Relative pronoun left out / preposition at the end.","explanation_ar":"حذف ضمير الوصل."},{"q":"In the souq, you can ___ souvenirs.","options":["take","do","buy","make"],"answer":"buy","explanation":"Travel collocation.","explanation_ar":"متلازمة السفر."},{"q":"Have lunch with a family ___ hospitality you will never forget.","options":["who","whose","who's","which"],"answer":"whose","explanation":"Relative clause (who / which / that / where / whose).","explanation_ar":"جملة الصلة."},{"q":"","options":["Shubra Palace is a building that it is more than a hundred years old.","Shubra Palace is a building that is more than a hundred years old.","Shubra Palace is a building where is more than a hundred years old.","Shubra Palace is a building who is more than a hundred years old."],"answer":"Shubra Palace is a building that is more than a hundred years old.","explanation":"Combining sentences correctly.","explanation_ar":"دمج الجمل."}]},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS5-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Prepare for the tourist information desk.","items":[{"q":"A place in your city where tourists can relax: ___","options":[],"answer":"Al-Rudaf Park is a place where tourists can relax.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses defining relative clauses, omitted relative pronouns, travel collocations, sentence combining (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A dish which visitors should try: ___","options":[],"answer":"Visitors should try saleeg, which is a creamy rice dish.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses defining relative clauses, omitted relative pronouns, travel collocations, sentence combining (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A person who can help tourists: ___","options":[],"answer":"The guides who work at the museum can help tourists.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses defining relative clauses, omitted relative pronouns, travel collocations, sentence combining (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A souvenir visitors can buy: ___","options":[],"answer":"The rose oil you can buy in the souq is a great souvenir.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses defining relative clauses, omitted relative pronouns, travel collocations, sentence combining (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"One travel tip for a visitor: ___","options":[],"answer":"Book your hotel early because the summer is busy.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses defining relative clauses, omitted relative pronouns, travel collocations, sentence combining (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS5-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write your visitor's guide.","items":[{"q":"Plan: city, 3 places (with where/which/that), 1 person (who/whose), 1 food or souvenir, 3 collocations.","options":[],"answer":"Jeddah; Al-Balad where old houses stand, the corniche which is great at sunset, the fountain that is very tall; fishermen whose boats leave at dawn; try the local seafood; go sightseeing, buy souvenirs, book a hotel","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses defining relative clauses, omitted relative pronouns, travel collocations, sentence combining (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write your guide in 7–8 sentences.","options":[],"answer":"Jeddah is a city where the desert meets the Red Sea. Go sightseeing in Al-Balad, where the old houses have wooden windows. The corniche is a place which is perfect at sunset. Don't miss the fountain that is one of the tallest in the world. You can meet fishermen whose boats leave at dawn. Try the local seafood they catch every morning. Buy souvenirs in the old souq. Book your hotel early in winter!","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses defining relative clauses, omitted relative pronouns, travel collocations, sentence combining (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Check: who/which/where/whose? repeated pronouns? collocations? Fix one mistake.","options":[],"answer":"I changed \"a place which you can see the sea\" to \"a place where you can see the sea\".","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses defining relative clauses, omitted relative pronouns, travel collocations, sentence combining (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Spin the Review Wheel","mechanic":"spin_wheel","rules":["A player spins the wheel and lands on a word.","Say what the word means and use it in a sentence with defining relative clauses, omitted relative pronouns, travel collocations, sentence combining (unit review).","Teammates check: meaning (1 point), grammar (1 point), pronunciation (1 point).","Highest total after 6 minutes wins."],"data":[{"cue":"destination","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"journey","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"landmark","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"souvenir","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"accommodation","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"luggage","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"cuisine","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"abroad","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"itinerary","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"sightseeing","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"tourist","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"local","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"explore","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"hospitality","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"A tour guide is a person ___ shows visitors around a place.","options":["where","who","whose","which"],"answer":"who","explanation":"Relative clause (who / which / that / where / whose).","explanation_ar":"جملة الصلة."},{"q":"Abha is a city ___ you can enjoy green mountains.","options":["whose","which","where","who"],"answer":"where","explanation":"Relative clause (who / which / that / where / whose).","explanation_ar":"جملة الصلة."},{"q":"","options":["The honey bought we in Al-Baha was delicious.","The honey we bought it in Al-Baha was delicious.","The honey which we bought it in Al-Baha was delicious.","The honey we bought in Al-Baha was delicious."],"answer":"The honey we bought in Al-Baha was delicious.","explanation":"Relative pronoun left out / preposition at the end.","explanation_ar":"حذف ضمير الوصل."},{"q":"","options":["That's the museum I told you about it.","That's the museum I told you about.","That's the museum I told you.","That's the museum where I told you about."],"answer":"That's the museum I told you about.","explanation":"Relative pronoun left out / preposition at the end.","explanation_ar":"حذف ضمير الوصل."},{"q":"We were late, so we ___ the bus.","options":["missed","lost","failed","left behind it"],"answer":"missed","explanation":"Travel collocation.","explanation_ar":"متلازمة السفر."},{"q":"Before you travel, ___ your hotel online.","options":["book","make","do","catch"],"answer":"book","explanation":"Travel collocation.","explanation_ar":"متلازمة السفر."},{"q":"","options":["Visit the gallery which its paintings show old Taif.","Visit the gallery who its paintings show old Taif.","Visit the gallery who's paintings show old Taif.","Visit the gallery whose paintings show old Taif."],"answer":"Visit the gallery whose paintings show old Taif.","explanation":"Combining sentences correctly.","explanation_ar":"دمج الجمل."},{"q":"How can we combine \"Try the coffee. They make it with cardamom.\"?","options":["Try the coffee they make it with cardamom.","Try the coffee where they make with cardamom.","Try the coffee they make with cardamom.","Try the coffee who they make with cardamom."],"answer":"Try the coffee they make with cardamom.","explanation":"Combining sentences correctly.","explanation_ar":"دمج الجمل."},{"q":"Our ___ to Al-Ula took four hours by car.","options":["travels","trips","travel","journey"],"answer":"journey","explanation":"Travel collocation.","explanation_ar":"متلازمة السفر."},{"q":"Which sentence would work best in a guide for visitors?","options":["Visit the old fort where overlooks the city, and try the local dates at the gate.","Visit the old fort which overlooks the city, and try the local dates they sell at the gate.","Visit the old fort which it overlooks the city, and try the local dates they sell them at the gate.","Visit the old fort who overlooks the city, and make the local dates at the gate."],"answer":"Visit the old fort which overlooks the city, and try the local dates they sell at the gate.","explanation":"Relative clause (who / which / that / where / whose).","explanation_ar":"جملة الصلة."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS5-IMG01","prompt":"A colorful travel leaflet \"Welcome to Taif!\" with photos of rose farms, a cable car over the mountains, Shubra Palace, a souq with honey and roses, and a smiling local family welcoming tourists. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A colorful travel leaflet \"Welcome to Taif!\" with photos of rose farms, a cable car over the mountains, Shubra Palace, a souq with honey and roses, and a smiling local family welcoming tourists"},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS5-IMG02","prompt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Travel and Culture”, speech bubbles left empty. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as teenagers aged 14–17, realistic proportions, modern school/city settings. Square 1:1.","alt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Travel and Culture”, speech bubbles left empty"},{"id":"GV-L06-U1-LS5-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Spin the Review Wheel”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Spin the Review Wheel"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS5-T1","script":"Taif is a city where the mountains meet the clouds. It's a perfect destination for tourists who want cool weather in summer. First, visit the rose farms which make Taif famous. The rose water they produce is sold all over the world. Next, take the cable car that goes down from Al-Hada through the mountains – the view is amazing! Then go sightseeing at Shubra Palace, a building that is more than a hundred years old. In the souq, you can buy souvenirs and try the local honey. Before you leave, have lunch with a family whose hospitality you will never forget.","voice":"en-US, natural young-adult voice","speed":"0.95"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L06-U1-LS5-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “A Visitor's Guide to My City: Language in Use”. Let’s learn defining relative clauses, omitted relative pronouns, travel collocations, sentence combining (unit review) together. Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Tourist information desk: Student A is a tourist visiting your city; Student B works at the information desk. The tourist asks 4 questions (Where can I…? What's a place where…?). The desk worker answers with relative clauses and travel collocations. Then swap. Today you use all of Unit 1: defining relative clauses, leaving out the relative pronoun, travel collocations and combining sentences. A visitor's guide recommends places (visit…, take…, try…), describes them (a city where…, a palace that…) and gives tips. Here are some examples. Al-Baha is a region where you can walk through juniper forests. Visit the market which opens every Friday. The guide who works at the fort is very friendly. Be careful! Some learners say: “Taif is a city which the mountains meet the clouds.” That’s not right. We say: “Taif is a city where the mountains meet the clouds.” Place → where. Quick check: what is wrong with “The rose water they produce it is famous.”? … Think! … It should be “The rose water they produce is famous.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can recommend places in my city to a visitor.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"0.95"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"An architect is someone ___ designs buildings.","options":["who","which","whose","where"],"answer":"who","explanation":"People → who (or that).","explanation_ar":"الأشخاص ← who."},{"q":"The Red Sea has coral reefs ___ are home to thousands of fish.","options":["whose","where","which","who"],"answer":"which","explanation":"Things and animals → which (or that).","explanation_ar":"الأشياء ← which."},{"q":"Madinah is the city ___ the Prophet's Mosque is.","options":["whose","where","which","who"],"answer":"where","explanation":"Places → where.","explanation_ar":"الأماكن ← where."},{"q":"I met a boy ___ grandfather was a famous poet.","options":["whose","which","who's","who"],"answer":"whose","explanation":"Possession → whose.","explanation_ar":"الملكية ← whose."},{"q":"","options":["The driver who he took us to the hotel was very kind.","The driver which took us to the hotel was very kind.","The driver whose took us to the hotel was very kind.","The driver who took us to the hotel was very kind."],"answer":"The driver who took us to the hotel was very kind.","explanation":"Don't repeat the pronoun after who/which.","explanation_ar":"لا تكرر الضمير."},{"q":"","options":["The scarf bought I in the souq is made of silk.","The scarf which I bought it in the souq is made of silk.","The scarf I bought it in the souq is made of silk.","The scarf I bought in the souq is made of silk."],"answer":"The scarf I bought in the souq is made of silk.","explanation":"Don't add an extra pronoun (it/him/them).","explanation_ar":"لا ضمير زائد."},{"q":"In which sentence can we leave out the relative pronoun?","options":["The dish that we ordered was spicy.","The dish that came first was spicy.","The plane which landed was late.","The waiter who served us was polite."],"answer":"The dish that we ordered was spicy.","explanation":"Object pronoun → you can leave it out.","explanation_ar":"المفعول ← يمكن حذفه."},{"q":"","options":["The woman which answered the phone was from the embassy.","The woman answered the phone was from the embassy.","The woman who answered the phone was from the embassy.","The woman who she answered the phone was from the embassy."],"answer":"The woman who answered the phone was from the embassy.","explanation":"Subject pronoun → you must keep it.","explanation_ar":"الفاعل ← لا يُحذف."},{"q":"Is this the hotel you stayed ___ last year?","options":["at it","in","in it","where"],"answer":"in","explanation":"Preposition at the end (stayed in, talked to).","explanation_ar":"حرف الجر في النهاية."},{"q":"We were so tired that we ___ in at the hotel and went straight to bed.","options":["checked","took","booked","packed"],"answer":"checked","explanation":"Choose the right verb partner (book, pack, catch, go…).","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة الصحيحة."},{"q":"Have you ever traveled ___?","options":["to abroad","abroad","in abroad","at abroad"],"answer":"abroad","explanation":"go abroad (no \"to\").","explanation_ar":"go abroad."},{"q":"The ___ from Riyadh to Jeddah by train is very comfortable.","options":["trips","journey","travels","travel"],"answer":"journey","explanation":"travel / trip / journey.","explanation_ar":"travel أو trip أو journey."},{"q":"My father went on a business ___ to Dubai.","options":["journey","travels","travel","trip"],"answer":"trip","explanation":"travel / trip / journey.","explanation_ar":"travel أو trip أو journey."},{"q":"","options":["How much luggage do you have?","How much luggages do you have?","How many luggage do you have?","How many luggages do you have?"],"answer":"How much luggage do you have?","explanation":"luggage is uncountable.","explanation_ar":"luggage غير معدودة."},{"q":"The style of cooking of a country is its ___.","options":["accommodation","hospitality","itinerary","cuisine"],"answer":"cuisine","explanation":"Choose the travel word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."},{"q":"A thing you buy to remember a place is a ___.","options":["landmark","destination","luggage","souvenir"],"answer":"souvenir","explanation":"Choose the travel word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى الصحيح."},{"q":"How can we combine \"We visited an island. Turtles lay their eggs there.\"?","options":["We visited an island which turtles lay their eggs.","We visited an island where turtles lay their eggs.","We visited an island where turtles lay their eggs there.","We visited an island who turtles lay their eggs."],"answer":"We visited an island where turtles lay their eggs.","explanation":"Places → where.","explanation_ar":"الأماكن ← where."},{"q":"","options":["I have a neighbor whose son is a pilot.","I have a neighbor who's son is a pilot.","I have a neighbor which son is a pilot.","I have a neighbor who his son is a pilot."],"answer":"I have a neighbor whose son is a pilot.","explanation":"Possession → whose.","explanation_ar":"الملكية ← whose."},{"q":"","options":["The bus who goes to the beach leaves every hour.","The bus that goes to the beach leaves every hour.","The bus leaves every hour that goes to the beach.","The bus that it goes to the beach leaves every hour."],"answer":"The bus that goes to the beach leaves every hour.","explanation":"Put the clause right after the noun and delete the repeated pronoun.","explanation_ar":"مكان الجملة وحذف الضمير."},{"q":"What is wrong with \"We loved the hotel which it had a big garden.\"?","options":["Nothing is wrong.","It needs a comma after hotel.","\"it\" should be deleted.","\"which\" should be \"who\"."],"answer":"\"it\" should be deleted.","explanation":"Don't repeat the pronoun after who/which.","explanation_ar":"لا تكرر الضمير."},{"q":"Which word replaces \"their\" when we join sentences?","options":["which","where","whose","who"],"answer":"whose","explanation":"Possession → whose.","explanation_ar":"الملكية ← whose."},{"q":"Choose the best sentence for a travel blog.","options":["We stayed in a village where people still bake bread in clay ovens there, and we tried the local cheese they make it.","We stayed in a village who people still bake bread in clay ovens.","We stayed in a village which people still bake bread in clay ovens, and we made the local cheese.","We stayed in a village where people still bake bread in clay ovens, and we tried the local cheese they make."],"answer":"We stayed in a village where people still bake bread in clay ovens, and we tried the local cheese they make.","explanation":"Places → where.","explanation_ar":"الأماكن ← where."},{"q":"How can we fix it? \"abha is a city which you can see clouds below you.\"","options":["abha is a city where you can see clouds below you.","Abha is a city which you can see clouds below you.","Abha is a city where you can see clouds below you.","Abha is a city where you can see clouds below you there."],"answer":"Abha is a city where you can see clouds below you.","explanation":"Places → where.","explanation_ar":"الأماكن ← where."},{"q":"Where did you buy that lantern? – ___","options":["I bought it in the souq where my uncle took us to it.","I bought it in the souq my uncle took us to it.","I bought it in the souq my uncle took us to.","I bought it in the souq who my uncle took us to."],"answer":"I bought it in the souq my uncle took us to.","explanation":"Preposition at the end (stayed in, talked to).","explanation_ar":"حرف الجر في النهاية."}],"theme_ar":"السفر والثقافة","essential_question_ar":"ماذا يمكن أن نتعلّم من زيارة أماكن وثقافات أخرى؟","can_do":"I can describe people, places and things with defining relative clauses, leave out the relative pronoun when it is an object, use travel collocations, and combine short sentences with who, which, that, where and whose.","can_do_ar":"أستطيع أن أصف الأشخاص والأماكن والأشياء بجمل الصلة المحدِّدة، وأحذف ضمير الوصل عندما يكون مفعولًا به، وأستخدم متلازمات السفر، وأربط الجمل القصيرة بـ who وwhich وthat وwhere وwhose."});
